share_chat.py — verify only. Never mint.
=========================================

WHAT THIS IS. A prepared patch for `~/ablit-central/bin/share_chat.py`, which
lives in a different repository, on a machine this could not be tested against.
It is the ablit-central half of the centralised-auth rebuild; the other half is
`server/auth/` in this repository. `server/auth/CUTOVER.md` is the runbook, and
this patch is step 6 of it. Do not apply it on its own — applied before
flamme-auth is up and reachable, it leaves the chat app redirecting to a sign-in
that does not exist.

WHERE IT WAS PREPARED AGAINST. `bin/share_chat.py` as of ablit-central's working
tree on alien18, 2026-09-04, 175989 bytes, the copy that also sits on mpcn0.
The chat app itself runs on auroraR16, which is not reachable from here, so this
has been compile-checked and read but never run. Diff it before you trust it.

HOW TO APPLY.

    cd ~/ablit-central
    git status                       # commit or stash first; you want a revert
    patch -p1 --dry-run < share_chat.py.patch
    patch -p1 < share_chat.py.patch
    python3 -m py_compile bin/share_chat.py

HOW TO ROLL BACK. `patch -R -p1 < share_chat.py.patch`, or `git checkout --
bin/share_chat.py` if it was clean when you started. The rollback is a genuine
rollback: nothing in this patch changes a stored format, a hash, a cookie name
or a file on disk, so the old code picks up exactly where it left off. The one
thing it does NOT undo is a rotated SESSION_SECRET — see CUTOVER.md step 9.

WHAT IT CHANGES, IN SIX PIECES.

 1. THE BUG, KILLED AT THE ROOT. The four lines that invented a random
    SESSION_SECRET when the environment had none are gone. In their place, a
    hard `SystemExit` if the key is missing OR is still the unexpanded
    `<python -c ...>` placeholder from .env.example. A chat app that cannot
    verify a session must not start: it is a private model with the door open,
    and "logins reset on restart" was never the real symptom — the real symptom
    was that every OTHER service silently stopped recognising anybody.

 2. `AUTH_URL` + `CENTRAL_AUTH` replace `OAUTH_ENABLED` as the gate on the
    custom-route path. The gate is no longer "is Google configured" (it never
    should have been); it is "does this instance delegate sign-in", which every
    deployment does. `TUNNEL=gradio` — local development — still gets Gradio's
    built-in form and its own users.conf, and `auth_fn` is labelled accordingly.

 3. `/login`, `/auth/password`, `/auth/google/*` and `/logout` become redirects
    to flamme-auth. They are kept rather than deleted because they are in
    bookmarks, in history and in older builds of the game. Note `/auth/password`
    answers **303**, not 302: a browser must turn that POST into a GET, because
    the password must not be forwarded to a machine that is no longer allowed to
    see one.

 4. `/logout` cannot do the job alone and says so. The cookie is now
    `Domain=.edeliverables.com`, and a `Set-Cookie` from
    abliterated.edeliverables.com can only clear a cookie scoped to
    abliterated.edeliverables.com. Only the issuer can revoke it. The
    `delete_cookie` that remains is for the OLD host-only cookie this app set
    before the cutover, which nothing else can reach either.

 5. `_session_user()` — a new reader that tries EVERY `ablit_session` cookie in
    the raw header and takes the first that verifies. `request.cookies` is a
    dict, so two cookies of the same name collapse into one and which survives
    is a coin toss. Two is the *normal* state of an already-signed-in browser
    for the seven days after the cutover, because the old host-only cookie sits
    in the jar next to the new domain-scoped one. Without this, roughly half of
    all requests from those browsers read as signed out. `baye.py` and
    `server/auth/auth.py` got the same change in the same release.

 6. `_login_html` is left in place and unreferenced, with a comment saying so.
    It moved to flamme-auth. It stays here so that reverting this patch is one
    command and not an archaeological dig.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CHANGE. The cookie name, the token format, the
hash format, `webauth.py`, or anything a user can see once they are signed in.
Every password in `/etc/flamme-auth/users.conf` is the same
`pbkdf2_sha256$200000$…` line it was in `conf/users.conf`, copied verbatim, so
every password that worked yesterday works today.

WHAT IT NEEDS IN THE ENVIRONMENT ON auroraR16 (or wherever the app is that day):

    SESSION_SECRET=<the value from /etc/flamme-auth/auth.env on mpcn0>
    AUTH_URL=https://edeliverables.com/auth      # this is also the default

The first is now load-bearing rather than optional, which is the entire point.

